// Title: Use of Wfc.exe
// Sigma ID: 49be8799-7b4d-4fda-ad23-cafbefdebbc5
// Status: test | Level: medium
// Author: Christopher Peacock @SecurePeacock, SCYTHE @scythe_io
// Date: 2022-06-01 | Modified: -
// ATT&CK: execution, stealth, T1127
// Description: The Workflow Command-line Compiler can be used for AWL bypass and is listed in Microsoft's
//   recommended block rules.
// False positives:
//   - Legitimate use by a software developer
// References:
//   - https://lolbas-project.github.io/lolbas/OtherMSBinaries/Wfc/
//   - https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/security/application-security/application-control/windows-defender-application-control/design/applications-that-can-bypass-wdac
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/05d1031f537e830b76da53bae2400c5814c89165/rules/windows/process_creation/proc_creation_win_lolbin_wfc.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language 12.4.1, live Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceProcessEvents
| where FolderPath endswith "\\wfc.exe" or ProcessVersionInfoOriginalFileName =~ "wfc.exe"
